<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Vixen — test run settings.

  This file exists for one reason, and it is worth writing down because the
  symptom is baffling and the fix looks unrelated to it.

  On macOS the Khronos validation layer installed by Homebrew enumerates and
  then refuses to load: `vkCreateInstance` returns ERROR_LAYER_NOT_PRESENT for a
  layer `vkEnumerateInstanceLayerProperties` has just listed. The cause is that
  Homebrew's layer manifest names its library by bare filename —

      "library_path": "libVkLayer_khronos_validation.dylib"

  — and the Vulkan loader resolves a bare name through dlopen, whose default
  search path on macOS is /usr/local/lib and /usr/lib. The dylib is in
  /opt/homebrew/lib, which is on neither. Homebrew's own caveat suggests
  VK_LAYER_PATH; that was measured and does *not* help, because VK_LAYER_PATH
  tells the loader where to find the *manifest* and the manifest was never
  missing. Pre-loading the dylib by absolute path from managed code does not
  help either, for the same reason: the loader still dlopens the bare name.

  DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH is the only lever, and dyld reads it once at process start,
  so it cannot be set from inside the test. Setting it here means `dotnet test`
  gets validation without anyone having to remember an export in a shell
  profile — which would otherwise make "are the validation layers on?" depend on
  which terminal the suite was launched from, and quietly answer "no" in CI and
  in the IDE.

  The variable is ignored on Windows and Linux, where it is also unnecessary:
  both resolve the layer library correctly on their own.

  Pass this file to `dotnet test` with its settings switch. (Spelling the switch
  out here is not an option: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment.)

  ⚠ ONE TARGET STILL READS THIS FILE, AND IT USED TO BE FOUR. `Test`,
  `GoldenImages` and `AffectedTests` run their assemblies through
  Microsoft.Testing.Platform now (#560), which does not read a run settings file
  at all — and would have said so in an MTP0001 *warning* rather than an error,
  leaving validation silently off. They call `Build.ExportLayerLibraryPath`
  instead, which sets the same variable on the process that starts the test
  executable; dyld reads it at process start either way. `Coverage` is the one
  still on VSTest, because its cobertura collector is a VSTest data collector,
  and it is the reason this file is still here.
-->
<RunSettings>

  <RunConfiguration>
    <EnvironmentVariables>
      <!--
        Both prefixes: /opt/homebrew/lib is Apple silicon, /usr/local/lib is
        Intel and is already on the default path, so naming it costs nothing and
        keeps the two machines' settings identical.
      -->
      <DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH>/opt/homebrew/lib:/usr/local/lib</DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH>

      <!--
        The marker that says a run's environment was applied at all, set by both
        mechanisms that apply one: this file, and `Build.ExportLayerLibraryPath`
        for the three targets that run under Microsoft.Testing.Platform and
        therefore never read this file.

        ⚠ It exists so that `RunSettingsTests` can tell "the mechanism ran and
        did not deliver" from "no mechanism ran". Without it that theory is red
        under a bare `dotnet test <one project>` — the command CLAUDE.md's
        working agreement recommends and the one an agent in a worktree is
        limited to — where nothing ever promised the variable, so the red meant
        only that the run was the documented one (#985).

        ⚠ And it is what stops an ambient `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` from a shell
        profile passing the theory: a marker nothing but these two mechanisms
        sets cannot be exported by accident, which is a stronger guard than the
        exact-value comparison it replaces — and that comparison was itself
        wrong, because `ExportLayerLibraryPath` PREPENDS to an inherited value.
      -->
      <VIXEN_TEST_SETTINGS_APPLIED>1</VIXEN_TEST_SETTINGS_APPLIED>
    </EnvironmentVariables>
  </RunConfiguration>

</RunSettings>
